patrone-task-manager (0.2.0) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Users: Sign out and Disconnect, from buttons above the list or an
    account's right-click menu, as in Windows. Each asks first ("Sign out
    Base? Unsaved work in their apps will be lost."). Signing out another
    account goes to logind (TerminateUser) with interactive authorization,
    so polkit asks for an administrator's password right then; signing out
    your own account goes through the desktop's own log-out. Disconnect
    ends only the sessions that show an account's desktop on a screen
    (TerminateSession), leaving the desktop running with its apps open. If
    the password isn't given or the request fails, it says so and nothing
    changes.
  * The Sessions column says what an account has: its desktop, whether
    it's shown on the screen, and terminal sign-ins. It used to count
    desktops that keep running without a screen as remote.

 -- William Daugherty <31995458+billsecond@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:05:00 -0500

patrone-task-manager (0.1.0) resolute; urgency=medium

  * First package: Patrone Task Manager, in the place of Ubuntu's Resources
    (Conflicts and Replaces). Pages: Processes (apps, background processes
    and system processes, each with its processes under it, and their
    processor, memory, disk and network use, shaded as they get busier),
    Performance (processors, memory, disks, network adapters and graphics
    cards that say how busy they are, each with a minute's graph), App
    history (processor time per app since the computer started, from the
    journal), Startup apps (a switch for each autostart entry), Users,
    Details (every process) and Services (start, stop and restart system
    services or your own).
  * End task, End process tree, Set priority, Open file location,
    Properties and Run new task; ending something other than an app asks
    first, and other accounts' processes go through pkexec.
  * Ctrl+Shift+Esc opens it: a custom shortcut in a new Patrone dconf
    database (/etc/dconf/db/patrone.d), read through a vendor profile in
    /usr/share/dconf/profile/user, from the next sign-in.

 -- William Daugherty <31995458+billsecond@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:15:00 -0500
